{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-352756",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let ${a}_{n}=\\frac{n!}{(n+4)!}$ and ${b}_{n}=\\frac{{(-1)}^{n}\\,n!}{(n+4)!}.$ Then ${a}_{n}$ is positive and decreasing for $n\\ge 1,$ and ${a}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ So, the series is convergent by the alternating series test.\n\nBecause our series is a convergent alternating series, the alternating series error bound applies. In particular, for the $6$th partial sum, it states that\n\n$$\n|S-{s}_{6}|<|{b}_{7}|=|\\frac{{(-1)}^{7}\\,7!}{(7+4)!}|=\\frac{7!}{11!}=\\frac{1}{8\\cdot 9\\cdot 10\\cdot 11}=\\frac{1}{7920}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=\\frac{1}{7920}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
